---
import Photo from "../components/Photo.astro"
import { longFormImage } from "../data/media"
---

<section
  id="long-form"
  class="bg-background py-16 sm:py-20 lg:py-28"
  aria-labelledby="long-form-heading"
>
  <div
    class="mx-auto grid w-full max-w-[calc(var(--container-copy)+14rem+3rem)] grid-cols-1 gap-10 px-container-x sm:px-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,var(--container-copy))_14rem] lg:items-start xl:px-14"
  >
    <article class="reveal min-w-0">
      <p
        class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase"
      >
        Technology — Long-form report
      </p>
      <h2
        id="long-form-heading"
        class="text-balance text-headline-2 leading-tight font-semibold text-foreground"
      >
        The quiet migration back to paper forms.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted italic sm:text-xl">
        Twelve public services digitized in the same decade. All twelve are,
        quietly, adding paper back in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-muted">
        Editorial Team ·{" "}
        <time datetime="2026-08-21">August 21, 2026</time> · 12 min read
      </p>

      <div
        class="mt-10 flex flex-col gap-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 sm:text-lg"
      >
        <p>
          The first office to admit it was a small transit authority two states
          over. Their online renewal form had a ninety-one percent completion
          rate in usability testing and a forty-four percent completion rate in
          the wild. The gap wasn't a bug. It was every account creation prompt,
          every password requirement, every session that expired while someone
          searched a drawer for a document number.
        </p>
        <p>
          Paper doesn't ask you to remember a password. It doesn't time out. It
          doesn't require a compatible browser, a stable connection, or a device
          newer than the one already sitting on the kitchen table. For a
          specific, recurring kind of transaction — renewal, registration,
          enrollment — that turns out to matter more than any interface
          improvement the digital form could offer.
        </p>

        <blockquote
          class="my-4 max-w-copy border-l-2 border-foreground/20 pl-5 text-2xl leading-snug font-medium text-foreground italic sm:text-3xl"
        >
          <p>
            "We didn't bring paper back because the software failed. We brought
            it back because paper never asked anyone to create an account."
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <Photo image={longFormImage} ratio="16 / 9" parallax class="my-2" />

        <p>
          None of the twelve services abandoned their digital forms — they run
          in parallel now, and the digital option still handles the majority of
          transactions. What changed is smaller and more telling: paper stopped
          being treated as the legacy fallback and started being treated as a
          first-class channel, with its own design review and its own completion
          targets.
        </p>
        <p>
          That distinction — legacy fallback versus first-class channel — is
          probably the actual story here. Nobody digitized a bad process and
          made it good by putting it on a screen. The services that are thriving
          are the ones that finally looked at both channels as equally
          deliberate choices, and stopped assuming the newer one was
          automatically the better one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <aside
      class="reveal flex flex-col gap-6 border-t border-border/50 pt-8 lg:border-t-0 lg:border-l lg:pt-0 lg:pl-8"
      aria-label="Side notes"
    >
      <div>
        <p
          class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase"
        >
          Editor's note
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Completion-rate figures throughout this piece are composites drawn
          from the twelve services studied, not a single source — reported this
          way at each service's request.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p
          class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-muted uppercase"
        >
          Related
        </p>
        <a
          class="block text-sm font-medium text-foreground no-underline hover:underline focus-visible:outline-3 focus-visible:outline-offset-3 focus-visible:outline-border"
          href="#dispatch"
        >
          The dashboard that stopped updating
        </a>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>
